(15, 'Classification', 'For how long is amoxicillin given for fast breathing only, in an area with little HIV?', 'For 3 days.', 2),
(16, 'Classification', 'What makes pneumonia severe (WHO)?', 'A general danger sign, central cyanosis or an oxygen saturation below 90%, or severe respiratory distress.', 2),
(17, 'Classification', 'What are the general danger signs?', 'Not able to drink or breastfeed, vomits everything, convulsions, and lethargy or unconsciousness.', 1),
(18, 'Classification', 'Is an antibiotic given for the classification "cough or cold"?', 'No.', 1),
(19, 'Hospital', 'Which antibiotics are given for severe pneumonia?', 'Ampicillin 50 mg/kg 6-hourly with gentamicin 7.5 mg/kg once daily, for at least 5 days.', 2),
(20, 'Hospital', 'A child with pneumonia does not improve after 48 hours of ampicillin and gentamicin. What is done?', 'The antibiotic is changed to ceftriaxone, and empyema is looked for.', 3),
(21, 'Hospital', 'How is suspected staphylococcal pneumonia treated?', 'With cloxacillin and gentamicin.', 2),
(22, 'Hospital', 'When is oxygen given in pneumonia?', 'When the oxygen saturation is below 90%, or with central cyanosis, grunting or inability to drink.', 1),
(23, 'Hospital', 'What is the best test for deciding on oxygen?', 'Pulse oximetry.', 1),
(24, 'Hospital', 'Why are intravenous fluids restricted in severe pneumonia?', 'Because of the risk of inappropriate ADH secretion; 70–80% of maintenance is given.', 3),
(25, 'Investigations', 'Is a chest X-ray needed in simple pneumonia?', 'No. It is needed only if the pneumonia is severe, not improving or complicated.', 2),
(26, 'Investigations', 'Which X-ray signs point to Staphylococcus aureus pneumonia?', 'Pneumatoceles, empyema, abscess and pneumothorax.', 2),
(27, 'Mycoplasma', 'What are the features of mycoplasma pneumonia?', 'A school child with a persistent dry cough, headache and few chest signs; the X-ray looks worse than the child.', 2),
(28, 'Mycoplasma', 'How is mycoplasma pneumonia treated?', 'With a macrolide (azithromycin).', 1),
(29, 'Mycoplasma', 'Why do penicillins not work against Mycoplasma?', 'It has no cell wall.', 2),
(30, 'Mycoplasma', 'What are the extrapulmonary features of mycoplasma infection?', 'Erythema multiforme, Stevens–Johnson syndrome, haemolytic anaemia, encephalitis and Guillain–Barré syndrome.', 2),
(31, 'Mycoplasma', 'What is the laboratory clue to mycoplasma infection?', 'Cold agglutinins.', 2),
(32, 'Empyema', 'When is empyema suspected?', 'When fever persists after 48 hours of antibiotics, with stony dullness and absent breath sounds.', 2),
(33, 'Empyema', 'What is the commonest cause of empyema?', 'Streptococcus pneumoniae.', 1),
(34, 'Empyema', 'What are the pleural fluid findings in empyema?', 'Pus or organisms on Gram stain, a pH below 7.2, a low glucose and a high LDH.', 2),
(35, 'Empyema', 'How is empyema treated?', 'With a chest drain and intravenous antibiotics; fibrinolytics or video-assisted thoracoscopic surgery (VATS) if it is loculated.', 2),
(36, 'Signs', 'What does a trachea pulled towards the dull side indicate?', 'Collapse of the lung.', 2),
(37, 'Signs', 'What does a trachea pushed away from a side with stony dullness indicate?', 'A large pleural effusion.', 2),
(38, 'Recurrent', 'What causes recurrent pneumonia in the same lobe?', 'A local cause: a foreign body, a tuberculous lymph node or a malformation.', 3),
(39, 'Recurrent', 'What causes recurrent pneumonia in different lobes?', 'Aspiration, cystic fibrosis, immunodeficiency or sickle cell disease.', 3),
